  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nNorthwesterly winds will ease early this evening with mainly\nclear skies expected tonight. These clear skies and light winds\nwill allow for strong radiational cooling. Forecast lows range\nfrom the lower 40s across the northern valleys with upper 40s to\nlower 50s in most other locations. Valley fog is possible \ntowards dawn, which may result in some localized visibility \nrestrictions. \n\nSurface high pressure will remain centered over New England on\nFriday into Friday night. This will result in mainly sunny skies\nand near average high temperatures into the lower 80s in most\nlocations south of the mountains. Radiational cooling is likely\nonce again Friday night with low temperatures falling into the\n40s and 50s, coolest in valley locations. \n\nPleasant conditions are then expected this weekend with\ncontinued low humidity and comfortable high temperatures into\nthe lower 80s and low temperatures mainly into the 50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper level low becomes stationary and deepens across Hudson\nBay by this weekend. A ridge passes through New England early \nthis weekend, and then as the upper level low strengthens, \ntroughing becomes more favored into the Northeast by early next \nweek.\n\nWith high pressure at the surface, a mainly sunny day with \nhighs in the upper 70s to mid 80s is expected for Saturday. The \nhigh slides offshore during the day, bringing in a steady \nsoutheasterly flow along the coast that knocks temps back by the\nafternoon hours. \n\nSunday sees a few more clouds and is likely cooler by a few \ndegrees in easterly flow as a plume of moisture mostly passes \noffshore. There is still a small chance that this could trend a \nlittle closer and bring a chance for showers on the coast on \nSunday, but at this point the forecast looks mainly dry.\n\nBy Monday and Tuesday, a slow moving system approaches from the\nwest. This brings an increasing chance for showers into western\nNew Hampshire on Monday, and then showers and storms are likely\nacross most of the area by Tuesday. Temperatures still look to \nwarm into the upper 70s to mid 80s each day, with any showers \nand storms primarily in the afternoon hours. The front \nassociated with the system likely takes until Wednesday to clear\nthrough New England, so a chance for showers and storms \ncontinues into Wednesday as well.\n\nBy late in the week, ridging likely begins to build back in as \nthe trough weakens, favoring a return to drier and slightly \nwarming conditions.",
